Day 16 of my DevOps Troubleshooting Series
🔴 Real-Time Jenkins Scenario: Docker Permission Denied
Imagine a Jenkins pipeline is working perfectly until it reaches the Docker build stage.
The pipeline suddenly fails with:
❌ permission denied while trying to connect to the Docker daemon socket
As a DevOps Engineer, what would you check?
🔍 My Troubleshooting Approach:
1️⃣ Check if Docker is running
systemctl status docker
If Docker is stopped:
sudo systemctl start docker
2️⃣ Check Docker socket permissions
ls -l /var/run/docker.sock
3️⃣ Check Jenkins user permissions
id jenkins
If Jenkins is not part of the Docker group:
sudo usermod -aG docker jenkins
4️⃣ Test Docker access using the Jenkins user
sudo -u jenkins docker ps
5️⃣ Check the Jenkins agent
If the pipeline is running on a Jenkins agent, make sure these checks are performed on the agent where the job is actually running, not just on the Jenkins controller.
🛠️ Possible Fix:
sudo usermod -aG docker jenkins
sudo systemctl restart jenkins
Then verify:
sudo -u jenkins docker ps
Once Docker access is confirmed, rerun the pipeline.
💡 Key Learning:
When Jenkins cannot access Docker, don't immediately modify the pipeline.
First check:
Docker Service → Docker Socket → Jenkins User → Permissions → Jenkins Agent
This kind of troubleshooting helps identify whether the issue is with the application, pipeline, agent, or underlying infrastructure
